<section aria-labelledby="introduction">
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
  <p>In Lesson 2 you created folders, organised files and saved your work. Today you meet the application you will use to write all your HTML and CSS: <strong>Visual Studio Code</strong>, usually called <strong>VS Code</strong>.</p>
  <p>By the end of the lesson you will know what VS Code is for, recognise its main areas, open your course folder in it, and create and save a file there. That prepares you for Lesson 4, where you write your first webpage.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="learn">
  <h2 id="learn">What you will learn</h2>
  <ul class="tick-list">
    <li>Define a code editor in simple language.</li>
    <li>Explain what VS Code is used for.</li>
    <li>Explain the difference between VS Code and a web browser.</li>
    <li>Open VS Code on a Windows computer.</li>
    <li>Identify the Explorer, the editor area, tabs, the menu bar and the status bar.</li>
    <li>Open your <code>Web_Development</code> folder, then create, save and reopen a file.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="explanation">
  <h2 id="explanation">Explanation</h2>

  <h3>What is a code editor?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>code editor</strong> is an application for writing and editing the instructions a computer or a browser reads. It looks a little like Notepad, but it is designed for code: it colours different parts of the code so they are easier to read, shows line numbers, and lets you see all the files of a project in one window.</p>
  <p><strong>VS Code</strong> is a free code editor made by Microsoft. Web developers use it to write HTML and CSS, among many other languages.</p>

  <h3>A code editor is not a browser</h3>
  <p>This is the most important idea in today’s lesson.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>VS Code</strong> is where you <em>write and edit</em> code.</li>
    <li>A <strong>web browser</strong>, such as Google Chrome or Microsoft Edge, is where you <em>open and view</em> the finished webpage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Think of a carpenter. VS Code is the workshop where the table is built; the browser is the showroom where people see the finished table. You will move between the two all the time.</p>

  <h3>The main areas of VS Code</h3>
  <p>When VS Code is open with a folder, the window has these areas. The exact look can vary slightly between versions.</p>
  <dl class="term-list">
    <div><dt>Menu bar</dt><dd>Along the top: <strong>File</strong>, <strong>Edit</strong>, <strong>View</strong> and so on. You will use <strong>File</strong> most, to open folders and save files.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Explorer</dt><dd>The panel on the left that lists the files and folders in the folder you opened. If it is hidden, click the top icon in the narrow bar on the far left (it looks like two sheets of paper).</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Editor area</dt><dd>The large area in the middle where the open file appears and where you type.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Tabs</dt><dd>Each open file has a tab above the editor area showing its name. Click a tab to switch files. A dot on a tab means the file has changes that are not saved yet.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Status bar</dt><dd>The thin bar along the bottom. Among other things, it shows the line your cursor is on and the type of file you are editing.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="demonstration">
  <h2 id="demonstration">Demonstration</h2>
  <h3>Step 1: Open VS Code</h3>
  <p>Click <strong>Start</strong>, type <em>Visual Studio Code</em> and open it. The first time, you may see a Welcome tab. You can close it with the × on its tab.</p>

  <h3>Step 2: Open your course folder</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Click <strong>File</strong> in the menu bar, then <strong>Open Folder…</strong></li>
    <li>Go to <strong>Documents</strong> and click once on <code>Web_Development</code> (do not open it).</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Select Folder</strong>.</li>
    <li>VS Code may ask whether you trust the authors of the files in this folder. You created this folder yourself, so choose <strong>Yes, I trust the authors</strong>. Only do this for folders you know are safe.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The Explorer now shows <code>HTML_Lessons</code>, <code>CSS_Lessons</code> and <code>HTML_Practice</code>, the folders you made in Lesson 2. Click the arrow beside <code>HTML_Lessons</code> to open it; you should see <code>My_First_Note.txt</code>.</p>

  <h3>Step 3: Create a file in VS Code</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>In the Explorer, click <code>HTML_Lessons</code> so it is selected.</li>
    <li>Right-click it and choose <strong>New File…</strong> (or click the small page-with-a-plus icon at the top of the Explorer).</li>
    <li>Type <code>about_me.txt</code> and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. The file opens in the editor area, and a tab with its name appears.</li>
    <li>Type one sentence about yourself. Notice the dot on the tab: the change is not saved yet.</li>
    <li>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd> to save. The dot disappears.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Step 4: Close and reopen it</h3>
  <p>Close the tab with its ×. Then click <code>about_me.txt</code> in the Explorer. It opens again with your sentence, because it was saved inside your course folder.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="practice">
  <h2 id="practice">Hands-on practice</h2>
  <div class="practice">
    <p class="practice-title">Exercise: From a closed computer to a saved file</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Close VS Code completely.</li>
      <li>Open it again and open your <code>Web_Development</code> folder using <strong>File → Open Folder…</strong></li>
      <li>Inside <code>HTML_Practice</code>, create a file called <code>practice.txt</code>.</li>
      <li>Type two sentences: what VS Code is for, and what a browser is for.</li>
      <li>Save it, close it and reopen it from the Explorer.</li>
      <li>Point to each of the five areas: menu bar, Explorer, editor area, tabs and status bar.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="check">
  <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="check-list">
    <li>What is a code editor?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between VS Code and a web browser?</li>
    <li>Where in VS Code can you see all the files in your project?</li>
    <li>What does a dot on a file’s tab mean?</li>
    <li>Which keyboard shortcut saves a file?</li>
  </ol>
  <details>
    <summary>Show the answers</summary>
    <ol>
      <li>An application used to write and edit code.</li>
      <li>VS Code is where you write and edit code; a browser is where you open and view the webpage.</li>
      <li>In the Explorer panel on the left.</li>
      <li>The file has changes that have not been saved.</li>
      <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
  </details>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="mistakes">
  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mistake</th><th scope="col">How to correct it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Opening a single file instead of the course folder.</td><td>Use <strong>File → Open Folder…</strong> and select <code>Web_Development</code>, so the Explorer shows the whole project.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Creating the new file in the wrong folder.</td><td>Click the right folder in the Explorer before choosing <strong>New File…</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Closing VS Code without saving.</td><td>Watch for the dot on the tab and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expecting VS Code to show the finished webpage.</td><td>VS Code shows the code. You will open the page in a browser in Lesson 4.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="summary">
  <h2 id="summary">Lesson summary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>VS Code is a code editor: the place where you write and edit code.</li>
    <li>A browser is where you view the result.</li>
    <li>The Explorer shows your project’s files; the editor area is where you type.</li>
    <li>Always open your <code>Web_Development</code> folder, not a single file.</li>
    <li>Save with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd> and check that the dot on the tab disappears.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

